WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.380
SANDER BOGDAN: Welcome to
Google Cloud Next 2021.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.670
In today's talk,
Secure and Reliable

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.930
Continuous Delivery to
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.540
In today's talk,
we're going to talk

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.265
through some of the challenges
of continuous delivery to GKE.

00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:27.640
We'll discuss the
principles that

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.690
can be applied to
address those challenges.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.690
We'll be introducing
Google Cloud Deploy.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.470
And finally, we'll show a demo.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.930
My name is Sander Bogdan,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.100
with Google Cloud.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.800
And I'm joined today by Bryan
Morgan, who's an engineering

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.070
manager with Google Cloud.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:51.070
So let's start off by
talking about challenges.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.380
Over the past 18 months,
we've had the opportunity

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.690
to speak with numerous
Google Cloud customers

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:00.430
about the continuous delivery
challenges they face with GKE.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.150
And from those conversations,
three themes emerge.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.870
The first theme is
cost of ownership.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.120
Defining robust
delivery pipelines,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.970
patching, pooling, and scaling
an organizational continuous

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:16.410
delivery capability can be
both difficult and complex.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.380
And even when an organizational
continuous delivery capability

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.930
has been established,
knowing what to measure

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.030
and how to optimize those
measurements for success

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.800
is often unclear.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.920
But in all situations,
continuous delivery investment

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.690
intensity can detract
from core business focus.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:38.370
So the second theme we observed
was around security and audit.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.250
Controlling releases as
they make their way out

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.980
to a production
environment, approving

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.970
releases for production, as
well as delivery pipeline access

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.510
control and audit were
repeated as fundamentally

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.610
necessary to modern
software delivery.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.800
And the final theme
was integrations.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.980
Continuous delivery capability
exists within software delivery

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.090
ecosystems.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.460
And because of that,
systems integrations

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.130
such as with continuous
integration, connecting

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.100
complex workflows such as
multi-step, multi-party

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.010
approvals, and advanced
networking requirements

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.550
demand continuous delivery
tooling flexibility as well as

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.110
modularity.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.610
And these three themes are best
summed up by recent customer

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:26.220
quote, and that quote
went something like this,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.680
"We cannot afford to be
innovating in continuous

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.520
delivery.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.550
We want an opinionated
product that provides

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.400
best practices out of box."

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.650
And while this quote is
associated with one customer,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.330
the reality is it
could be assigned

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.130
to any number of customer
conversations that we've had.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:42.630
All right.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.080
Now that we've talked
about the challenges,

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.247
I'd like to talk through
some of the principles that

00:02:46.247 --> 00:02:49.760
can be applied to
addressing these challenges.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.100
So there are four.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.200
The first principle is Easy--

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.360
make it easy to define
robust delivery pipelines,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.200
to onboard delivery
pipelines, scale them,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.640
and to apply best practices
as part of their setup.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.227
And we believe the best way to
do this is through a managed

00:03:11.227 --> 00:03:12.810
opinionated service,
and Bryan's going

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.680
to be talking more
about that later.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.920
The second principle is Control.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.680
Continuous delivery
tooling must facilitate

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.930
manual and automated
release control

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.216
as it makes its way out through
a production environment.

00:03:27.216 --> 00:03:30.630
So that includes activities
like promotion, rollback,

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.440
as well as approvals.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.423
The third principle is Security.

00:03:35.423 --> 00:03:37.340
And what that means is
platform administrators

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.070
must have the ability to
restrict delivery pipeline

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.230
definition and activities.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.180
They need to be able to
control who is doing what,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.035
where, and when.

00:03:47.035 --> 00:03:48.410
And when an audit
is needed, that

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.440
should be handily available too.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.560
And the final principle is
about providing insights.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.330
So continuous
delivery capability

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.940
must provide insights
both collectively

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.750
as well as individually for
delivery pipeline progressions,

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.740
activities, and opportunities
to further optimize delivery

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:10.055
stability as well
as release velocity.

00:04:10.055 --> 00:04:11.430
So again, the four
principles are

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.643
make it easy-- to make
it easy to onboard,

00:04:13.643 --> 00:04:15.060
make it easy to
scale, and make it

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.370
easy to maintain
continuous delivery tooling

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.540
using best practices.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.953
The second is provide control.

00:04:20.953 --> 00:04:23.370
Releases should be controllable
as they make their way out

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.510
through a production
environment.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:27.560
The third principle
is security-- control

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.120
who can do what, where,
and when with audit.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.540
And finally, provide
insights to further optimize

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.698
your continuous
delivery practices.

00:04:37.698 --> 00:04:39.740
So now that I've talked
through these principles,

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.160
I'd like to hand
it over to Bryan,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.690
who's going to talk to
how Google Cloud applies

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.220
these principles.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.880
Bryan?

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.740
BRYAN MORGAN: Thanks, Sander.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.020
Continuous delivery
capabilities frequently

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.630
exist within a software
development lifecycle

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.040
ecosystem.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.090
This implies flexibility and
modularity, as Sander noted,

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.700
as a delivery
tooling requirement.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:06.120
For GCP customers, connecting
Google and third-party tooling

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.280
that intersects with
your delivery processes

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.710
is a necessity, particularly
when you already

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.800
have continuous integration,
approval workflows,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.162
and associated test automation.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.330
We have found that GCP
Kubernetes customers typically

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.450
mature in their usage
of GKE over time.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.180
Starting out, customers may
have a single config that

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.820
targets a single
cluster, but this usually

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:34.330
grows into multi-config,
multi-cluster usage.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.720
From there, teams often find
that application configuration

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.460
should be standardized
and source

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.000
code controlled, allowing them
to templatize configuration

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.690
changes.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:48.450
Finally, advanced customers find
tools such as KEP and Kustomize

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:50.520
to be extremely
useful in allowing

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.000
them to make increasingly
sophisticated and automated

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.790
configuration changes
across environments.

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:01.640
Most software delivery
pipelines can be thought

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.710
of as a three-step process--

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.070
define, manage, and deliver.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:09.320
Because usage of
GKE is a journey,

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.980
we advocate using Skaffold for
the local development loop.

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.680
From there, Skaffold
profiles, in combination

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.690
with the managed continuous
delivery service,

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:20.870
can be used to define delivery
pipelines and orchestrate

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.400
releases.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.620
Finally, that service
can actuate the release

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.045
to GKE target clusters.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.740
Up until this point, we've
discussed the challenges,

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.380
principles, and application
of those principles

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:37.320
for continuous
delivery into GKE.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.330
Now, I'd like to step
through the key features

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:42.030
of our recent public preview
launch of Google Cloud

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:45.630
Deploy, GCP's continuous
delivery service for Google

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:46.770
Kubernetes Engine.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.570
We have engineered
Google Cloud Deploy

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.910
to be a scalable managed
service that enables

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.460
continuous delivery to GKE.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.100
Cloud Deploy relies on
a ConfigOS code model

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.830
with declarative manifests
for configuring the delivery

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.420
pipelines.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.390
In addition, it performs
GKE manifest rendering

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.640
and deployment on your behalf.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.560
Cloud Deploy additionally
supports a number

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.800
of security features,
including a discrete IEM

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.360
model, direct integration
to GCP Cloud audit logs,

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.050
manual approvals as part of
a deployment process flow,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.570
and even execution on
Cloud Build private pools

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.570
for customers that desire
security and control

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.770
over their environments.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.010
Finally, Cloud Deploy
has integration points

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:35.130
to enable both Google and
third-party delivery-related

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.520
tooling and process
integrations.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.860
I'd like to briefly walk
you through the experience

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.360
before I go into our demo.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.460
In this first slide, you can
see that Google Cloud Deploy

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.430
organizes CD workflows
around pipelines,

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.440
which can be
monitored and measured

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.320
using the GCP Cloud
console as well

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.380
as our gcloud command-line
interface and API.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.520
Next, in the Delivery
Pipeline Details view,

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.500
a pipeline's rollouts
can be controlled

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.720
through promotions
and approvals, which

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.700
are again available
via the Cloud Console

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.520
UI and the gcloud CLI.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.710
You'll also note a
delivery pipelines release

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:18.380
history is accessible
for similar review

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:19.175
and taking action.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.320
Users with appropriate
permissions

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.780
can then proceed with
a release promotion

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.060
to evaluate configuration
changes that

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:31.030
are included with the
release to be deployed.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.100
The Cloud Console user
interface supports

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:36.580
direct visual differentials to
aid in the release progression

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:37.795
decision-making process.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.059
I'd like to now demonstrate
the use of Google Cloud Deploy

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:46.735
to deploy an application to a
series of GKE target clusters.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.060
In this demo, I will
step through the process

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:57.190
of creating a GCP Cloud Deploy
release, deploying to staging,

00:08:57.190 --> 00:09:00.665
then approving a
production rollout.

00:09:00.665 --> 00:09:02.540
Today, I'm going to demo
the new Cloud Deploy

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:03.710
service from Google.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.690
We'll start with
a GitHub repo that

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:10.040
contains two applications that
will build two containers.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:12.373
That application is in
this example directory,

00:09:12.373 --> 00:09:13.790
and you'll see
there's a container

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.570
called leeroy-app and a second
container called leeroy-web.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.390
Both containers include their
own Kubernetes deployment

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:24.470
manifest as well as
a Skaffold config

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:26.690
that defines how
those containers will

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:27.650
be built and deployed.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:29.150
You can see here
that we're planning

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:33.800
to use the standard kubectl
dplyr to deploy the two GKE

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.150
clusters.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.660
If I take a look at GKE
and my Cloud console,

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.150
you can see that I've already
pre-created three clusters--

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:43.100
a test, staging, and prod.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.720
So those are already
ready and waiting for me.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:50.390
So the first step I want to
do is to go into Cloud Shell.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:51.830
I've already pulled
the repo down.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.865
So I want to do a Skaffold
build and upload-- building

00:09:54.865 --> 00:09:58.220
will upload those containers
to Artifact Registry.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:58.730
All right.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.160
We can see the container
build is finished.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.610
They have been uploaded.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.000
I'm going to go check out
my Artifact Registry just

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.150
to verify.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:06.650
Yep.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:09.200
We can see now just
now two containers

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.650
have been uploaded,
leeroy-app and leeroy-web.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:16.070
So for my next step, I
want to actually now set up

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:17.480
my Cloud Deploy pipeline.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:19.790
My containers are
built. They are ready.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.690
So let's take a look at
what that looks like.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:23.930
Within my repo, you can
see I have a declarative

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.450
manifest for my pipeline.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:28.680
And it looks just like
this, and it's very simple.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.680
I give it a name, which
I've called it "web-app"

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:32.150
with a description.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.370
And then it really just
defines three stages--

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:35.870
test, staging, and prod.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:38.150
The order in which
these are defined in

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:40.070
are basically the
directional flow

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:43.520
that the pipeline
will promote through.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.830
So let me go into
my Cloud Shell,

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:48.755
and I'm going to apply that
manifest to Cloud Deploy.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:52.650
All right.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.600
You can see it's
now been applied.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.350
I can go check out my Cloud
Deploy user interface.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.210
I'm going to do a refresh here.

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:01.710
All right.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:05.590
Now, we have a web pipeline,
and it's-- as we mentioned,

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:08.820
it's planning to promote
from test to staging to prod.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:12.130
Those are actually resources as
well that need to be created.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.180
And you can see I have those
defined declaratively as well.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.310
I have a target-test,
target-staging,

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:18.720
and target-prod.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.090
I've defined those in
separate manifests.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:22.860
So I'm going to run
those, and now that

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.440
will create each of these
targets in Cloud Deploy one

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:26.310
at a time.

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:29.280
All of these could have been
added to a single manifest.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:30.420
I just opted not to.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:34.950
All right.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:42.420
So I've created the test
target, the staging target,

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:44.670
and the production target.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.590
At this point, I could
do one of two things.

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:50.040
The first step I'll take
is just to manually create

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.760
a release in Cloud Deploy.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.780
So I'm going to go ahead and
create our first release.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:01.920
And we're going to
call it "web-app-001."

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:04.170
And you can see now that
we've created the release,

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.200
and it started a rollout
to the first stage

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.930
in our pipeline,
which we call test.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:17.500
If I take a look at
that, you can see

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:19.647
the rollout has been queued.

00:12:19.647 --> 00:12:20.230
It's starting.

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:22.450
And this is what the user
interface looks like.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:27.640
We show that there's a
test, a staging, and a prod.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:28.450
Rollout is queued.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:30.820
If you notice here,
between staging and prod,

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.350
it says "0 pending."

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.830
And what that's referring
to is zero approvals.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:39.010
And it knows that because if
we take a look at our prod

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:42.990
manifest, we configured
it to require approval.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.240
So we'll talk about
approvals in a little bit.

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:48.240
But effectively,
Cloud Deploy is aware,

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.080
and it's expecting an approval
before it allows that promotion

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.050
to that final target.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.702
While this pipeline is
queued, we're going to go in,

00:12:57.702 --> 00:12:59.160
and I'll show you
as well that I've

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:02.370
created a Cloud Build trigger
that will automatically

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.652
look for changes.

00:13:04.652 --> 00:13:07.110
And this is-- for those that
are familiar with Cloud Build,

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.000
this is looking at my Git repo.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.880
And any time a change happens
to that repo, a commit is made.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.560
It's going to build
a new container,

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.080
upload it to Artifact Registry.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:19.830
And then you can see
here, the second step

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.450
is it's going to then
create a new release.

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:24.150
So we can test that out.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:27.330
I will go into my
Visual Studio code.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:30.150
I have a sample app here.

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:35.910
Let's just call it
"Cloud Next 2021."

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:42.650
We're going to update the
comment here, apply the change,

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:43.820
"Updated comment."

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:51.158
We can see that
trigger is running now.

00:13:51.158 --> 00:13:52.700
And what that trigger
is going to do,

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.890
it's going to update the
containers in the Artifact

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:55.393
Registry.

00:13:55.393 --> 00:13:56.810
And then when
complete, it's going

00:13:56.810 --> 00:13:59.420
to kick off a brand-new
release in Cloud Deploy.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:13:59.970
OK.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.450
Our trigger has
successfully run,

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:06.140
which should mean that a Cloud
Deploy release has been kicked

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:09.792
off, and we see that it has.

00:14:09.792 --> 00:14:11.250
The test has been
deployed, and now

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:13.060
let's say we want
to do a promotion.

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:15.570
So we're going to go ahead
using the user interface

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.080
and force the promotion to--

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:22.890
kick off a promotion
to the staging target.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:23.390
OK.

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:26.700
Our promotion to
staging was successful.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.400
So now we really
have just one step

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.300
left-- that's to
promote to production.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.900
But if you recall,
production also

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.910
does require a manual approval.

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:37.470
So we're going to kick
off the promotion.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:40.850
And you can see now we
have one pending review.

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:42.740
I'll click the Review option.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:46.220
I'm going to go in there,
and I can see that, oh, look,

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.980
I have a rollout that
does need an approval.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.140
I do have the
appropriate permissions,

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.210
so I can go in there
and review that.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.010
At this point, I'm
ready to approve it.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:56.480
I know what changed.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.210
I can see the differences.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.550
And so I'm going to approve.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.910
I'm going to go
back to my pipeline.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:07.000
Our promotion to
prod was successful.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.010
And you can see now
we have successfully

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:13.420
promoted an automated release
through test, staging,

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:14.470
and production.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:16.960
This release was built
off of the GitHub

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:19.690
trigger using Cloud Build.

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:23.680
And this really showcases
the end-to-end CI/CD pipeline

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.390
using both Cloud Build
and Cloud Deploy.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:29.430
To learn more about
Google Cloud Deploy,

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:33.230
visit us at
cloud.google.com/deploy.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:35.517
Now back to you, Sander.

00:15:35.517 --> 00:15:37.350
SANDER BOGDAN: I'd like
to thank you, Bryan,

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.340
for presenting this
talk with me today,

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.120
as well as all of you for
attending Google Cloud Next

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:43.970
2021.

